{"type": "message", "id": "b929708f", "parentId": "a1aadf90", "timestamp": "2026-04-10T14:01:55.264Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**E — Gonadal mosaicism**\n\nHere's the reasoning:\n\n**Pedigree analysis:**\n- Generation I: Unaffected parents (grandfather + grandmother)\n- Generation II: Three children — II-1 (affected male, deceased, the older brother), II-2 (unaffected female), II-3 (middle unaffected male), and II-4 (the patient, marked with *, unaffected)\n- Generation III: III-1 (affected male — the 6-year-old nephew with severe muscle weakness), child of II-2 and II-3\n\n**Key clues:**\n1. The disease affects males (II-1 died at 13 from heart failure — consistent with Duchenne muscular dystrophy)\n2. The nephew (III-1) is affected — his mother is II-2\n3. **The patient's mother (Generation I female) is NOT a somatic carrier** — genetic testing confirmed this\n4. Yet two of her children (II-1 affected, and II-2 who passed the mutation to III-1) carry the mutation\n\n**Why gonadal mosaicism?**\n- The grandmother has the mutation only in her germline (gonadal) cells, not in somatic cells — explaining why somatic testing is negative\n- Some of her eggs carried the DMD mutation, others didn't\n- The patient (II-4) received an egg without the mutation → he is unaffected\n- II-1 and II-2 received eggs with the mutation → disease manifested\n\nThis is the classic scenario for **gonadal mosaicism**: a mother tests negative on somatic genetic testing but has multiple affected children because the mutation exists only in her germline."}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "openrouter", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6", "usage": {"input": 2199, "output": 391, "cacheRead": 15291, "cacheWrite": 730, "totalTokens": 18611, "cost": {"input": 0.006597, "output": 0.005865, "cacheRead": 0.0045873, "cacheWrite": 0.0027375, "total": 0.0197868}}, "stopReason": "stop", "timestamp": 1775829704638, "responseId": "gen-1775829704-8